  <section id="status" class="sec">
    <span class="sec-num">01</span>
    <h2>Where the cycle actually is right now</h2>
    <p class="lede">There is no single "internship season." There are separate calendars running at different speeds, and the honest answer to "am I late?" depends entirely on which one you are standing in.</p>

    <h3>Finance — front office</h3>
    <p>Most bulge-bracket and elite boutique Summer 2027 windows opened between December 2025 and March 2026 and have closed. That is the bad news, and it is real. The good news is that the cycle is not over: Adventis tracked 776 Summer 2027 postings from 109 firms through the end of July 2026, and expects roughly 600–650 more postings over the following six months, with around 24 firms still to post their first role in August and September. Middle-market banks, asset managers, corporate finance and Canadian bank co-ops all sit in the part of the cycle that is still ahead of you.</p>

    <h3>Consulting</h3>
    <p>MBB has compressed hard. McKinsey's Summer Business Analyst intern window closed 29 March 2026 and its U.S. full-time Business Analyst application closed 11 August 2026. Bain runs two Associate Consultant Intern windows for this cycle — 29 March and 31 August 2026 — which means the second one closes this week. BCG's undergraduate Summer Associate window for the Class of 2027 has passed; the sophomore-level round is expected in September. Big 4 advisory is the lane that is genuinely opening rather than closing.</p>

    <h3>Tech, AI and data</h3>
    <p>This is where you have the most runway. Applications open in waves from July 2026 into early 2027. Amazon and Databricks tend to post first. Google's software engineering intern posting historically opens around mid-October and stays up for a matter of weeks, not months — if you are targeting it, the alert matters more than the deadline. Almost nothing in tech has a fixed close date, which is exactly why applying in week one of a posting beats applying well in week five.</p>

    <h3>Engineering, hardware and core</h3>
    <p>Aerospace, defence, energy, manufacturing and automotive roles are live now and stay live into winter. These are consistently under-covered by the general software trackers, which is both the problem and the opportunity.</p>

    <div class="callout">
      <span class="k">The rule that matters most</span>
      <p>A third-party tracker is a discovery tool, never the source of truth. Confirm eligibility, location, deadline and application status on the employer's own site before you spend an hour tailoring anything.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

