          <dt>Watch-out</dt><dd class="warn">Convenient is not complete. Keep alerts running on employer and school portals as well.</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <span class="tag alt">Pay research</span>
        <h4><a href="https://www.levels.fyi/internships/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Levels.fyi Internships</a></h4>
        <dl>
          <dt>Best for</dt><dd>Researching intern compensation, housing stipends and company-specific history before you negotiate or rank offers.</dd>
          <dt>Coverage</dt><dd>Salary reports, role requirements and selected application links.</dd>
          <dt>Watch-out</dt><dd class="warn">Figures may be from prior cycles or other locations. It is not a live deadline tracker.</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Using trackers without wasting your evening</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><b>Subscribe, do not refresh</b><span>Turn on GitHub watch notifications, RSS or email alerts for the two feeds you actually trust. Manually reloading a repo is how people lose a week.</span></li>
      <li><b>Build role-family alerts, not company alerts</b><span>"Software engineer intern 2027," "data science intern 2027," "quantitative developer intern," "hardware intern," "process engineering intern." Companies you have not heard of are where the odds are.</span></li>
      <li><b>Check eligibility before you tailor</b><span>Location, graduation window, work authorization, sponsorship, academic term and degree level can disqualify you in ten seconds. Do that check first, not after writing a cover letter.</span></li>
      <li><b>Apply on the employer's own site</b><span>Aggregator copies can be cached, redirected or stale. If the official application exists, use it.</span></li>
      <li><b>Log the open date, not just the deadline</b><span>Under rolling review, when a role opened predicts your odds better than when it closes.</span></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

