    <p>The honest framing for first and second years: you are not going to win a competitive junior-summer seat right now, and you do not need to. What you need is one piece of real evidence — a shipped project, a research term, a small-company internship, a program on this list — that makes your junior-year application concrete instead of aspirational.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- 09 -->
  <section id="intl" class="sec">
    <span class="sec-num">09</span>
    <h2>If you are an international student</h2>
    <p class="lede">This is the section most recruiting guides skip. Work authorization is not a footnote at the end of your application — it determines which half of the postings are real for you, and finding that out early saves entire weekends.</p>

    <ul class="checks">
      <li><span><b>Filter by authorization before you filter by anything else</b>A posting that says "must be authorized to work without current or future sponsorship" is a no, regardless of how good the fit is. Building that check into step one of your workflow is the highest-leverage thing in this whole guide for you.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Never infer sponsorship from a tracker</b>Trackers frequently mislabel or omit sponsorship status. Read the employer's own wording on the posting, and answer application questions truthfully — a wrong answer here follows you.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Know your own status precisely</b>Whether you can work off-campus, how many hours during a study term, whether a co-op work permit is required for your program, and how your permit interacts with a full-time summer term. Get this from your institution's international student advising office, not from a group chat. Rules change.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>A Canadian co-op work permit is usually program-tied</b>If your degree requires a work placement, the co-op permit process is separate from your study permit and takes time. Start it well before you have an offer in hand, not after.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Weight Canadian employers more heavily than your peers do</b>The U.S. postings on most trackers will disproportionately exclude you. Section 04 is where your realistic volume lives — plus school-exclusive postings, which are open to you on the same terms as everyone else in your program.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Research programs and open-source have cleaner rules</b>Academic and research placements often have eligibility criteria that are explicit rather than buried, which makes them faster to qualify yourself against.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Do not disclose more than the form asks</b>Answer the authorization questions accurately and completely, and leave it there. Your cover letter is not the place to pre-emptively explain your immigration status.</span></li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout neutral">
      <span class="k">Not immigration advice</span>
      <p>I am an international student, not an immigration consultant or lawyer. Everything above is a research and prioritization workflow. For anything that determines your actual status, go to your institution's designated international student advisors or a licensed professional, and verify against official government sources.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 10 -->
  <section id="alerts" class="sec">
    <span class="sec-num">10</span>
    <h2>Set up alerts that actually work</h2>
    <p class="lede">Under rolling review, the gap between a role going live and you finding out is the whole game. An alert closes that gap from weeks to hours and takes about a minute to set up.</p>

    <ul class="checks">
      <li><span><b>Employer alerts</b>Create accounts and job alerts on the careers pages of your top 20–30 employers. This is tedious for one evening and then works for a year.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Platform alerts</b>LinkedIn, Simplify, TalentEgg, Prosple and your school portal, all using the same role-family keywords so results stay comparable.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>GitHub and RSS</b>Watch the two trackers you trust rather than refreshing them. Notifications land in your inbox; refreshing lands in your evening.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Calendar blocks</b>Two 20-minute scans a week now, three a week through September and October. Put them in the calendar or they will not happen.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Email hygiene</b>One professional address, checked daily including spam, with employer domains and assessment providers whitelisted. Online assessments expire, and they land in spam more often than you would think.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- 11 -->
  <section id="system" class="sec">
    <span class="sec-num">11</span>
    <h2>The application operating system</h2>
    <p class="lede">The difference between "I saw the role" and "I applied well" is a repeatable workflow. Seven steps, same every time.</p>

    <ol class="steps">
      <li><b>Discover</b><span>A tracker, employer page, school portal or referral surfaces the role. Log it immediately, even if you do not act today.</span></li>
      <li><b>Verify</b><span>Confirm it is live, eligible, correctly located and matched to your graduation window. For international students, authorization check goes here.</span></li>
      <li><b>Prioritize</b><span>Classify A, B or C — dream fit, strong fit, or low probability and low interest. Your A list should be short enough that you can genuinely tailor for it.</span></li>
      <li><b>Tailor</b><span>Adjust the top third of your resume, your skills line and two or three bullets to the role family. Not the whole document, and not five keyword swaps.</span></li>
      <li><b>Apply</b><span>For rolling or unstated deadlines, submit within 24–72 hours of discovery. Not at the final deadline, which under rolling review is often after the pipeline is full.</span></li>
      <li><b>Log</b><span>Open date, deadline type, application date, status, contact and next action. Six fields, one row, thirty seconds.</span></li>
      <li><b>Follow up</b><span>Networking gives you context and visibility. It does not replace applying, and a referral to a role you never submitted to is worth nothing.</span></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <!-- 12 -->
  <section id="tracker" class="sec">
    <span class="sec-num">12</span>
    <h2>Minimum viable application tracker</h2>
    <p class="lede">A spreadsheet is enough. These columns are what prevent missed assessment deadlines and accidental duplicate submissions, which are the two failure modes that actually cost people interviews.</p>

    <div class="tblwrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Identity</th><th>Timing</th><th>Pipeline</th><th>Follow-up</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Company<br>Role<br>Location<br>Application link<br>Role family<br>Sponsorship OK?</td>
            <td>Open date<br>Deadline<br>Deadline type<br>Date applied<br>Days from open</td>
            <td>Status<br>Assessment due<br>Interview stage<br>Outcome<br>Reason / notes</td>
            <td>Contact<br>Last touchpoint<br>Next action<br>Follow-up date<br>Referral status</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      <span class="k">Deadline types — the field people skip</span>
      <p><strong>Fixed</strong> is a hard close date. <strong>Priority</strong> means earlier review or preferred consideration. <strong>Rolling</strong> means reviewed as received. <strong>Unstated</strong> means assume rolling and move within 72 hours. Recording which type you are dealing with is what turns a list into a strategy.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 13 -->
  <section id="resume" class="sec">
    <span class="sec-num">13</span>
    <h2>Resume readiness</h2>
    <p class="lede">Your resume needs to be finished before the wave, not during it. Every hour spent rewriting it in October is an hour you are not applying.</p>

    <ul class="checks">
      <li><span><b>One page, single column</b>For most undergraduate applications, unless the employer explicitly asks otherwise. Two columns break more parsers than people realize.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>A strong top third</b>Education, most relevant experience and role-specific skills visible without scrolling. Assume six seconds of attention on the first pass.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Quantified bullets</b>Lead with the action, name the tool or method, close with impact or scale. "Built" is weaker than "built X in Y, cutting Z by N%."</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Role-family versions</b>Maintain separate finance/strategy, software/data and engineering/operations variants. Three files, not thirty.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>No fake tailoring</b>Swapping five keywords without changing the evidence underneath is not tailoring, and experienced reviewers can tell.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>File hygiene</b>Firstname_Lastname_Resume.pdf, every hyperlink tested, exported as PDF so formatting survives the upload.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

