  <!-- 15 -->
  <section id="qc" class="sec">
    <span class="sec-num">15</span>
    <h2>Quality control before you apply</h2>
    <p class="lede">Fast applications only help when the application is accurate and you are actually eligible. Ninety seconds of checking beats an hour of tailoring for a role you cannot take.</p>

    <ul class="checks">
      <li><span><b>Eligibility</b>Graduation window, degree level, location, term length, GPA requirements, and whether the posting accepts international students.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Work authorization</b>Read the employer's own wording. Do not infer sponsorship from a tracker, and answer application questions truthfully.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Duplicate roles</b>The same job appears under multiple cities and requisition IDs. Avoid accidental duplicate submissions unless the employer explicitly permits multiple locations.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Scam check</b>Apply through an employer domain or a trusted school portal. Treat interviews conducted over personal messaging apps, requests for money, and cheque or equipment schemes as disqualifying.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Assessment deadlines</b>The application deadline and the online assessment deadline are frequently different dates. Log both the moment you apply.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Save the evidence</b>Print the posting to PDF before it closes. You will want the exact wording when you prepare for the interview three months later.</span></li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout">
      <span class="k">Bottom line</span>
      <p>Start now, but do not panic. Front-office finance and MBB are largely past for Summer 2027, and pretending otherwise helps nobody. Big tech, AI, quant, Big 4, core engineering and the entire Canadian co-op calendar are ahead of you. The winning combination has not changed: early discovery, official verification, a finished resume, and disciplined tracking.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

